# CompTIA A+ — Security

Which malware type encrypts your files and demands payment?
---
Ransomware
~ Spyware
~ Rootkit
~ Worm
---
Recovery without paying means restoring from offline backups.

Which malware type spreads across a network on its own, with no user action?
---
Worm
~ Virus
~ Trojan
~ Keylogger
---
A virus needs a host file and a user to run it. A trojan needs the user to install it.

Which malware type disguises itself as legitimate software?
---
Trojan
~ Worm
~ Rootkit
~ Adware
---
Named for the horse. It does not self-replicate, it relies on you installing it.

Which malware type hides deep in the OS to conceal itself and other malware?
---
Rootkit
~ Spyware
~ Ransomware
~ Botnet
---
It can intercept the very system calls a scanner would use to find it, so detection often needs a boot-time scan.

Which attack sends fake emails that imitate a trusted sender to steal credentials?
---
Phishing
~ Shoulder surfing
~ Tailgating
~ Brute force
---
Spear phishing targets a specific person. Whaling targets executives.

What is tailgating?
---
Following an authorized person through a secured door
~ Watching someone type their password
~ Searching trash for documents
~ Redirecting a user to a fake website
---
Shoulder surfing is the watching, dumpster diving is the trash.

Which wireless security protocol is the current strongest?
---
WPA3
~ WPA2
~ WPA
~ WEP
---
WEP and WPA with TKIP are broken. WPA2-AES remains acceptable where WPA3 is unavailable.

Which encryption does WPA2 pair with?
---
AES
~ TKIP
~ RC4
~ DES
---
TKIP was WPA's stopgap over WEP's RC4 and is deprecated.

A password plus a code from your phone is an example of what?
---
Multifactor authentication
~ Single sign-on
~ Least privilege
~ Federation
---
Something you know plus something you have. Two passwords would still be one factor.

Which principle gives users only the access their job requires?
---
Least privilege
~ Zero trust
~ Defense in depth
~ Acceptable use
---
Standard user accounts for daily work, admin rights only where needed.

What does a screen privacy filter defend against?
---
Shoulder surfing
~ Phishing
~ Keyloggers
~ Evil twin access points
---
It narrows the viewing angle so only the person in front can read the display.

Which NTFS behavior applies when a file is copied to a different NTFS volume?
---
It inherits the destination folder's permissions
~ It keeps its original permissions
~ It loses all permissions
~ It becomes read-only
---
Moving within the same volume keeps the original permissions. Copying always inherits.

What should be done with a drive before donating an old computer?
---
Wipe it with a secure erase tool
~ Delete the user folder
~ Empty the Recycle Bin
~ Quick-format it
---
Deleting and quick formatting leave the data recoverable. Physical destruction is the alternative for sensitive drives.
